media: sun50i-h713-hdmirx: the HDCP state as a sysfs file, the firmware's last HDCP line through cpu_comm

HDCP 2.2 works in practice - the key comes out of the device's own storage at boot and the firmware
takes it (h713-hdcp-key, step 20 of the init sequence) - but nothing on the board could say whether a
protected source really negotiates, short of a photo of the screen. The firmware owns the receiver's
HDCP side entirely and has no callback for it, so this reads what it leaves behind.

A file "hdcp" in the hdmirx platform device's sysfs directory - a dev_groups attribute of the driver,
so the core adds it once the probe is through, and userspace reaches it from the video node as
/sys/class/video4linux/videoN/device/hdcp - five lines: the version negotiated, whether a session
stands, the two keys, the firmware's own last HDCP log line. Sysfs and not debugfs because the release
defconfig has no debugfs and the readout has to work on the shipped kernel; one file and not five
because the lines are one measurement, the verdict and the raw bits come out of the same register
read. The bits come out of the LINK window the driver already reads byte-wise for the audio status
(0136): +0x422 [0] is the 2.2 session the firmware's HdcpRx thread clears at every AKE_Init
(sub_8B13D970) and sets when the last message of a successful exchange has been handled - that is
"authenticated" for 2.2; +0x3c0 [2] and +0x161 [7] are written at AKE_Init and say an exchange has
begun; +0x41a [2] is set once the 2.2 key has been taken; +0x41b/+0x41c is the RxStatus the source
polls (message size, READY, REAUTH_REQ); +0x093 [0] is the 1.4 key, which no boot of ours loads
(doku/112). A finished 1.4 authentication is an interrupt the firmware clears in the same breath
(HdmiRx_HDCP14_ISR_Handle_ByLink, 0x8b13d1a8) and leaves nothing to read; the file says so instead of
guessing. Nothing here is key material, only status bits. The debugfs status file gets a one-line
summary.

The firmware's last word comes from its log ring, which cpu_comm already knows the address of (it is
the board's, allwinner,elog, and differs on the HY300 Pro): cpu_comm_elog_last() walks the mode-1 ring
backwards from the write pointer and returns the newest line containing a word, without touching the
firmware's read pointer. The firmware logs its 2.2 success ("HDCP Process cost tick") and its failures
at its error level, so they are in the ring at the default level; a 1.4 pass is an INFO line and is not.

The init sequence remembers what became of the key step (handed over, skipped, not reached) for the
same file. h713-tv ctl hdcp copies the five lines through (FB-HDCP22, FB-HDCP22b).
---
--- a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c
@@ -74,6 +74,7 @@
 #include <linux/seq_file.h>
 #include <linux/slab.h>
 #include <linux/spinlock.h>
+#include <linux/sysfs.h>
 #include <linux/unaligned.h>
 #include <linux/v4l2-dv-timings.h>
 #include <linux/workqueue.h>
@@ -430,6 +431,53 @@
 #define H713_LINK_RAW_ON	0xff
 
 /*
+ * The HDCP status bits of the receiver, read only, for the sysfs file
+ * "hdcp" and the status file's one line. The display firmware owns the
+ * receiver's HDCP side entirely: HDCP 1.4 is a hardware block whose finished
+ * authentication arrives as an interrupt the firmware clears at once
+ * (HdmiRx_HDCP14_ISR_Handle_ByLink, 0x8b13d1a8, "port%d HDCP1.4 Pass!" at
+ * INFO level), so a 1.4 pass leaves nothing to read afterwards; HDCP 2.2 is
+ * a state machine in the firmware (its HdcpRx thread) and the hardware
+ * carries the DDC messages and the session. What stays readable, all bytes
+ * in the LINK window:
+ *
+ *  +0x093 [0]  the 1.4 key is in. 0 on our boots: nothing loads it before
+ *              the firmware starts (doku/112).
+ *  +0x41a [2]  the 2.2 key has been taken: the receiver advertises 2.2
+ *              (set right after "HdmiRx_HDCP22_LoadKey ok!!").
+ *  +0x41b, +0x41c [1:0]  the 2.2 RxStatus message size the source polls;
+ *              +0x41c [2] READY, [3] REAUTH_REQ.
+ *  +0x3c0 [2], +0x161 [7]  written at every AKE_Init (sub_8B13D970): a 2.2
+ *              exchange has begun. A 1.4 pass clears both.
+ *  +0x422 [0]  a 2.2 session stands: cleared at AKE_Init, set by the
+ *              handler of the last message (SKE_Send_Eks). For 2.2 this bit
+ *              is "authenticated". A 1.4 pass clears it too.
+ *  +0x06d [0]  link 0 connected (sub_8B13E538).
+ *
+ * Addresses and functions: display.bin, database
+ * umbau/work/FB-HDCP22/ida/display.bin.i64, FB-HDCP22 report and
+ * tables/hdcp-observables.csv.
+ */
+#define H713_LINK_HDCP14_KEY		0x093
+#define H713_LINK_HDCP14_KEY_LOADED	BIT(0)
+#define H713_LINK_LINKSTAT		0x06d
+#define H713_LINK_LINKSTAT_LINK0	BIT(0)
+#define H713_LINK_HDCP22_HOLD		0x161
+#define H713_LINK_HDCP22_HOLD_BIT	BIT(7)
+#define H713_LINK_HDCP22_MODE		0x3c0
+#define H713_LINK_HDCP22_MODE_BIT	BIT(2)
+#define H713_LINK_HDCP22_CAP		0x41a
+#define H713_LINK_HDCP22_CAP_BIT	BIT(2)
+#define H713_LINK_HDCP22_RXSTATUS_LO	0x41b
+#define H713_LINK_HDCP22_RXSTATUS_HI	0x41c
+#define H713_LINK_HDCP22_RXSTATUS_SIZE_HI	GENMASK(1, 0)
+#define H713_LINK_HDCP22_RXSTATUS_READY	BIT(2)
+#define H713_LINK_HDCP22_RXSTATUS_REAUTH	BIT(3)
+#define H713_LINK_HDCP22_SESSION	0x422
+#define H713_LINK_HDCP22_SESSION_ON	BIT(0)
+#define H713_HDCP_LOG_LINE		160	/* of the firmware's log line */
+
